/* Kelime Defteri: ek okuma parçaları (B1-C1), İngilizce Vize okuma metinlerine benzer. Paragraflar çevrilmez; yalnızca soru ve şık çevirileri READINGS_TR içindedir. */
(function(){var A=[{"id":"kitchen6","t":"Cooking Together","lv":"B1","g":[["community kitchen","topluluk mutfağı"],["from scratch","sıfırdan"],["expiry date","son kullanma tarihi"],["allergy","alerji"],["belong","ait olmak"],["shift","vardiya"]],"p":["In many cities, a quiet change is taking place in the way people eat. Instead of cooking alone at home or buying ready meals, more and more people are joining community kitchens. These are shared spaces where neighbours meet once or twice a week to prepare a meal together and then eat it at one big table. Some kitchens are run by charities, while others are organised by the residents themselves.","Nora, a 28-year-old nurse, joined a community kitchen in her neighbourhood last year. She had recently moved to a new city and knew nobody there. \"I worked long shifts and usually ate a sandwich standing up,\" she says. \"At the kitchen, I learned to make soup from scratch and, more importantly, I made friends.\" Now she goes every Thursday evening and often brings a recipe from her grandmother.","The kitchens also help people save money. The food is usually bought in large quantities, so it is cheaper, and the ingredients are shared among everyone. Many kitchens use vegetables that supermarkets cannot sell because they look strange or are close to their expiry date. In this way, the projects reduce food waste as well as the cost of a meal.","Of course, there are challenges. Organisers need a suitable place, and the rules about food safety are strict. Volunteers must wash their hands, label allergies and keep the kitchen spotless. Some people also find it difficult to cook in a large group because everyone has a different idea about how much salt or spice a dish needs. \"We argue about garlic every week,\" laughs one volunteer.","Still, most members agree that the benefits are greater than the problems. Cooking together gives people a reason to leave the house, talk to others and try new foods. As one organiser says, \"A meal is never only about food. It is about sitting together and feeling that you belong.\""],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Ready meals are replacing home cooking in most cities.","Charities should run all the kitchens in a city.","Community kitchens help people eat well, save money and meet others.","Learning to cook is more important than making friends."],"a":2,"e":"The text covers friendship, lower costs and shared meals."},{"q":"Why did Nora join the community kitchen?","o":["She wanted to become a professional cook.","She had moved to a new city and knew nobody there.","Her hospital asked staff to cook together.","She was tired of her grandmother's recipes."],"a":1,"e":"She had recently moved and knew nobody."},{"q":"How do the kitchens save money?","o":["They get free food from restaurants every evening.","They grow all of their vegetables in their own gardens.","They ask members to bring food from home.","They buy food in large amounts and use ingredients that shops cannot sell."],"a":3,"e":"Bulk buying and unsellable vegetables lower the cost."},{"q":"In paragraph 4, \"spotless\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["carefully organised","completely clean","recently repaired","brightly lit"],"a":1,"e":"Spotless means without any dirt or marks."},{"q":"What problem do some volunteers mention about cooking in a group?","o":["There are not enough tools for everyone.","Members disagree about how to season the food.","The meals take too long to prepare.","Many members do not know how to cook."],"a":1,"e":"They argue about garlic, salt and spice."},{"q":"What does the organiser's last comment suggest?","o":["Meals are more enjoyable when they are expensive.","Most members join mainly to learn cooking skills.","People value the feeling of togetherness as much as the food.","Eating alone is healthier than eating in a group."],"a":2,"e":"\"Feeling that you belong\" is the key idea."}]},{"id":"swim6","t":"Learning to Swim as an Adult","lv":"B1","g":[["anxiety","kaygı"],["embarrassed","utanmış"],["instructor","eğitmen"],["flexible","esnek"],["technique","teknik"],["float","suda yüzmek / yüzer kalmak"]],"p":["Most people learn to swim as children, but a surprising number of adults never do. The reasons vary: some grew up far from water, others had a frightening experience, and many simply felt too embarrassed to ask for lessons later in life. For these people, standing at the edge of a pool can be a source of real anxiety.","Tomas, a 42-year-old accountant, was one of them. When he was seven, he fell into a lake and had to be pulled out by his uncle. After that, he avoided water for thirty-five years. \"I told my family that I didn't like the beach,\" he says. \"The truth was that I was afraid.\" The turning point came when his daughter started swimming lessons and asked him to join her in the pool.","Tomas signed up for an adult beginners' class at his local sports centre. The first lesson was difficult. He held on to the side of the pool and could not even put his face in the water. However, the instructor, a patient woman called Lucia, did not rush him. She explained that fear is a natural reaction and that progress is measured in small steps. In the first month, the class practised only breathing and floating.","Instructors say that adult learners face special challenges. Their bodies are less flexible than children's, and they worry about what other people think. On the other hand, adults are usually more determined, and they understand instructions better. \"Children copy what they see,\" says Lucia. \"Adults ask why, and that helps them to improve their technique.\"","After six months, Tomas could swim twenty-five metres without stopping. Last summer, he swam in the sea for the first time. \"I cried a little,\" he admits, \"but nobody could see because my face was wet anyway.\" He now recommends lessons to anyone who is still afraid. \"It's never too late. The hardest part is walking through the door.\""],"q":[{"q":"Why do some adults never learn to swim?","o":["They believe swimming is dangerous for older people.","Adult lessons are not available in most cities.","They have no interest in sports.","They lived far from water, were frightened, or felt too embarrassed to ask for lessons."],"a":3,"e":"The first paragraph lists three kinds of reasons."},{"q":"What made Tomas afraid of water?","o":["He nearly drowned in a swimming pool.","He was laughed at during a school lesson.","He fell into a lake when he was a child.","His uncle forced him to swim."],"a":2,"e":"He fell into a lake at the age of seven."},{"q":"What made him decide to take lessons?","o":["His daughter asked him to join her in the pool.","His doctor advised him to exercise more.","He wanted to swim on holiday with friends.","His family complained about his fear."],"a":0,"e":"The turning point was his daughter's request."},{"q":"How did Lucia help Tomas at the beginning?","o":["She asked him to swim a short distance on the first day.","She gave him a private lesson every week.","She let him progress slowly with simple exercises.","She told him to ignore his fear."],"a":2,"e":"She did not rush him; the class only practised breathing and floating."},{"q":"What does Lucia mean by \"Adults ask why\"?","o":["Adults are afraid of making mistakes.","Adults often question the instructor's methods.","Adults need more time to learn than children.","Adults want to understand how a technique works."],"a":3,"e":"Understanding helps adults improve their technique."},{"q":"What does Tomas suggest at the end?","o":["Swimming in the sea is easier than in a pool.","Taking the first step is the most difficult part.","Only young children should learn to swim.","Lessons should be free for adults."],"a":1,"e":"\"The hardest part is walking through the door.\""}]},{"id":"tiny6","t":"Living Small","lv":"B1","g":[["square metre","metrekare"],["liberating","özgürleştirici"],["afford","gücü yetmek"],["carbon footprint","karbon ayak izi"],["belongings","eşyalar"],["legal","yasal"]],"p":["For most of the twentieth century, the idea of a \"dream home\" meant more space: more bedrooms, a bigger garden and a double garage. Recently, however, a growing number of people have chosen the opposite. They live in tiny houses of less than thirty square metres, many of which are built on wheels so that they can be moved.","Ivy, a 31-year-old web designer, built her tiny house in a friend's garden. It has a kitchen, a bathroom, a sleeping area above the stairs and a small desk by the window. \"At first, I worried that I wouldn't have enough room for my things,\" she says. \"So I sold or gave away about three quarters of what I owned. It felt strange, then liberating.\"","There are practical reasons for the trend. Houses in many cities have become so expensive that young people cannot afford to buy one. A tiny house costs a fraction of the price, and its bills are much lower because it needs less energy to heat. Some owners also like the idea of leaving a smaller carbon footprint.","Living small has disadvantages, too. Guests have to sit close together, and storing things is a constant challenge. In many places, it is also difficult to find legal land where a tiny house can be placed, because local rules often require a minimum size for homes. Couples sometimes find that two people in such a small space need a lot of patience.","Nevertheless, supporters say the lifestyle teaches valuable lessons. \"You stop buying things just because they are on sale,\" Ivy explains. \"You ask yourself whether you really need something, and usually the answer is no.\" For her, the biggest benefit is not the money she saves, but the freedom to spend her time on what matters to her."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Tiny houses are the best solution to the housing crisis.","Large homes are disappearing from modern cities.","Building a tiny house is easy for anyone.","Some people choose very small homes for practical and personal reasons."],"a":3,"e":"The text gives reasons, problems and benefits of tiny houses."},{"q":"What did Ivy do before moving into her tiny house?","o":["She got rid of most of her belongings.","She asked her friend to design the house.","She rented out her old home.","She bought furniture designed for small spaces."],"a":0,"e":"She sold or gave away three quarters of her things."},{"q":"Why are tiny houses attractive from a financial point of view?","o":["They can be sold for a higher price later.","They do not have to pay local taxes.","They are cheaper to buy and to heat.","They are built from recycled materials."],"a":2,"e":"The price is a fraction of a normal house and bills are lower."},{"q":"In paragraph 2, \"liberating\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["causing a feeling of loss","needing a lot of effort","giving a feeling of freedom","making life more complicated"],"a":2,"e":"Ivy felt free after getting rid of her things."},{"q":"What difficulty is mentioned about the location of tiny houses?","o":["Local rules often make it hard to find legal land.","There is little land available in the countryside.","Neighbours often complain about the noise.","Tiny houses cannot be moved easily."],"a":0,"e":"Many places have minimum-size rules for homes."},{"q":"What does Ivy see as the greatest benefit of living small?","o":["Saving enough money to buy a bigger house.","Owning fewer things than her friends.","Living closer to her workplace.","Having the freedom to spend time on what matters to her."],"a":3,"e":"She says freedom matters more than the money saved."}]},{"id":"dream6","t":"Why Do We Dream?","lv":"B2","g":[["vivid","canlı, net"],["discard","atmak, bir kenara bırakmak"],["hypothesis","varsayım"],["distressing","üzücü, sıkıntı verici"],["by-product","yan ürün"],["sceptical","kuşkucu"]],"p":["Every night, whether we remember it or not, we dream. A typical person spends about two hours dreaming, which adds up to roughly six years over a lifetime. Yet scientists are still debating a basic question: why? Dreams have fascinated philosophers for thousands of years, but it is only in the last century that researchers have been able to study sleeping brains in detail.","The most vivid dreams occur during a stage called REM sleep, when the eyes move rapidly and the brain is almost as active as when we are awake. One influential theory suggests that this is when the brain sorts through the day's experiences, deciding which memories to keep and which to discard. In experiments, students who slept after learning a list of words remembered more of them the next day than students who stayed awake, which supports the idea that sleep strengthens memory.","A second theory focuses on emotions. Dreams, according to this view, work like a kind of overnight therapy. Because the chemicals associated with stress are reduced during REM sleep, the brain can revisit painful experiences without feeling their full force. People who have lost a loved one or survived a frightening event often dream about it repeatedly, and these dreams usually become less distressing over time.","Some researchers are more sceptical. They argue that dreams may simply be a by-product of the brain's activity, a kind of random noise that the mind turns into stories. According to this \"activation-synthesis\" hypothesis, the content of a dream has no special meaning. Supporters of this view point out that dreams are often illogical: we accept impossible events without surprise and rarely notice that we are dreaming.","Whatever their purpose, dreams can influence waking life. Artists and scientists have reported solving problems or finding ideas in their sleep, and some therapists use dreams as a starting point for discussion. Perhaps the most sensible conclusion is that dreaming serves more than one function. As technology improves, researchers hope to track brain activity so precisely that the mystery will finally be solved."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the text?","o":["To explain how to remember dreams more clearly.","To compare different explanations for why people dream.","To prove that dreams can predict future events.","To describe the stages of sleep in detail."],"a":1,"e":"The text presents memory, emotion and random-activity theories."},{"q":"What does the experiment with students suggest?","o":["Learning is easier in the evening than in the morning.","Students who sleep less remember more words.","Dreaming improves problem-solving skills.","Sleep helps the brain to store new information."],"a":3,"e":"Students who slept remembered more words."},{"q":"According to the second theory, why do disturbing dreams become less upsetting over time?","o":["The brain deletes unpleasant memories completely.","People learn to wake up before the dream becomes frightening.","Dreams become shorter as people get older.","Chemicals linked to stress are lower during REM sleep."],"a":3,"e":"The brain can revisit painful memories with less stress."},{"q":"In paragraph 4, \"sceptical\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["excited by a new idea","unwilling to listen to others","doubtful about whether something is true","certain that an idea is correct"],"a":2,"e":"Sceptical researchers question the earlier theories."},{"q":"Why do supporters of the \"activation-synthesis\" idea mention illogical dreams?","o":["They believe illogical dreams are more creative.","They think this shows dreams have no hidden meaning.","They want to show that dreams are rare.","They think dreams are controlled by the dreamer."],"a":1,"e":"Illogical events suggest random brain noise."},{"q":"What does the writer conclude?","o":["Dreams are only meaningless brain activity.","The mystery of dreams has already been solved.","Dreaming probably has several different purposes.","Dreams are important only for memory."],"a":2,"e":"\"Dreaming serves more than one function.\""},{"q":"Which of these is NOT mentioned as a possible influence of dreams on waking life?","o":["Dreams can give artists new ideas.","Dreams can help scientists solve problems.","Dreams can improve a person's physical health.","Dreams can give therapists something to discuss."],"a":2,"e":"Health benefits are not mentioned in paragraph 5."}]},{"id":"street6","t":"Vandalism or Culture?","lv":"B2","g":[["vandalism","kamu malına zarar verme"],["mural","duvar resmi"],["commission","sipariş, görevlendirme"],["developer","müteahhit, geliştirici"],["impermanence","geçicilik"],["auction","açık artırma"]],"p":["Not long ago, painting on a public wall without permission was seen as little more than vandalism. Today, the work of street artists hangs in galleries, sells for millions at auction and attracts tourists from around the world. This change has led to a lively debate: should street art remain a rebellious act, or can it become a respectable part of the art world?","Defenders of street art argue that it brings colour and meaning to neglected places. In many cities, councils now invite artists to decorate blank walls, abandoned factories and tunnels. In one industrial area of Bristol, a series of murals reduced the amount of graffiti tagging by almost half, as local people felt more proud of their neighbourhood and less willing to damage it.","Critics, however, point out that legal commissions take away what made street art special. Part of its appeal came from its risk and its unexpected appearance; once it is organised by officials, they say, it loses its voice. Others worry about a phenomenon known as \"artwashing\", in which property developers pay for attractive murals to make an area fashionable, only for rents to rise and long-term residents to be forced out.","Then there is the question of ownership. When a famous artist paints on a wall, who does the picture belong to: the artist, the building owner or the public? In several cases, owners have sold walls with the artwork still on them, while artists have complained that they received nothing. Because the paintings are exposed to weather and are sometimes covered over by other artists, they are also temporary by nature.","Many artists accept this impermanence as part of the point. \"A wall is not a canvas,\" says one painter, \"it is a conversation. Someone will answer me by painting over my work, and that's fine.\" Whether it is vandalism or culture, street art has changed how many of us look at the city around us, and it encourages us to see public space as something that anyone can shape."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main topic of the text?","o":["How to become a successful street artist.","The history of graffiti in industrial cities.","The changing status of street art and the arguments around it.","The best cities in the world for murals."],"a":2,"e":"The text weighs both sides of a debate."},{"q":"What happened in the industrial area of Bristol?","o":["Tourists stopped visiting because the area became too popular.","The council banned all painting on walls.","Murals led to less damage because residents felt proud of the area.","Property prices fell after artists moved in."],"a":2,"e":"Graffiti tagging dropped by almost half."},{"q":"What worry do critics express about legal commissions?","o":["Artists are paid too little for their work.","Officially organised art may lose its independent character.","Murals are often badly painted.","Councils choose only famous artists."],"a":1,"e":"Critics say organised art \"loses its voice\"."},{"q":"What does \"artwashing\" refer to?","o":["Using art to make an area fashionable so that property values rise.","Cleaning graffiti off public walls.","Selling street art to private collectors.","Copying famous paintings onto buildings."],"a":0,"e":"Developers pay for murals; rents then go up."},{"q":"Why is ownership a problem?","o":["Artists cannot sign work done in public.","Building owners must pay to keep the picture.","The public is not allowed to photograph murals.","It is unclear who has rights to a picture painted on someone else's wall."],"a":3,"e":"Owners have sold walls and artists got nothing."},{"q":"What attitude does the painter in the last paragraph show?","o":["He is angry when people paint over his work.","He accepts that his work may be covered by others.","He prefers painting on canvas to walls.","He believes only famous artists should paint walls."],"a":1,"e":"\"Someone will answer me by painting over my work, and that's fine.\""},{"q":"Which statement best describes the writer's approach?","o":["The writer presents both sides without choosing one.","The writer strongly supports legal murals.","The writer thinks street art is a waste of money.","The writer argues that street art should be banned."],"a":0,"e":"Both defenders and critics are given space."}]},{"id":"gig6","t":"The Gig Economy","lv":"B2","g":[["flexibility","esneklik"],["pension","emeklilik maaşı"],["insurance","sigorta"],["minimum wage","asgari ücret"],["entitle","hak vermek"],["contract","sözleşme"]],"p":["Ten years ago, few people had heard of \"gig work\". Today, millions earn money by delivering food on bicycles, driving passengers or completing small online tasks, with each job arranged through a smartphone app. This \"gig economy\" has changed how people think about employment, and opinion is divided on whether the change is good.","For many workers, the main attraction is flexibility. Gig workers decide when and how long to work, which suits students, parents with young children and anyone who needs extra income. Mara, a 24-year-old university student, delivers meals at weekends. \"I can switch off the app when I have an exam,\" she says. \"In a regular job, my manager would probably not be so understanding.\"","Yet this freedom comes at a price. Most gig workers are classed as self-employed, so they receive no paid holidays, sick pay or pension contributions from the companies whose apps they use. If they fall ill or have an accident, they simply stop earning. Their income can also be unpredictable: demand rises and falls, and the payment for each job may change without warning. Some workers find that after paying for fuel, insurance and repairs, their real hourly pay is below the minimum wage.","Governments are struggling to respond. In some countries, courts have ruled that certain gig workers should be treated as employees, which would entitle them to basic rights. Companies argue that such rules would force them to cut jobs and raise prices, and that workers themselves value independence. Surveys show that opinions differ: many people appreciate the freedom, but a majority also say they would like more protection.","Economists suggest that the answer may lie in a middle path, such as a flexible contract that still provides basic security. Whatever solution is found, the gig economy is unlikely to disappear. As more of our lives move online, the question is not whether this type of work will exist, but how fairly it will be organised."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Gig work offers flexibility but also creates serious insecurity.","Gig companies pay their workers better than traditional employers.","Smartphone apps have made all jobs easier.","Governments have banned most gig work."],"a":0,"e":"The text balances freedom against lack of protection."},{"q":"Why does Mara like gig work?","o":["She can stop working when she has exams.","She earns more than regular employees.","Her manager helps her to study.","She works full time and gets paid holidays."],"a":0,"e":"She can switch off the app during exams."},{"q":"What does \"self-employed\" mean for gig workers here?","o":["They receive a fixed salary every month.","They are guaranteed a minimum number of jobs.","They work only for companies in their own country.","They are responsible for their own income and get no employer benefits."],"a":3,"e":"No paid holidays, sick pay or pension contributions."},{"q":"Why might some workers' real pay be below the minimum wage?","o":["Companies reduce payments at the end of each month.","They spend too many hours waiting for the app to load.","Customers rarely give tips to delivery workers.","They must pay for costs such as fuel and insurance."],"a":3,"e":"Fuel, insurance and repairs reduce real pay."},{"q":"In paragraph 4, \"entitle\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["force someone to do something","give someone the right to something","give a title to someone","allow someone to avoid something"],"a":1,"e":"Employee status would give them basic rights."},{"q":"What do the surveys show?","o":["People like the freedom but also want more protection.","Most people want gig work to be banned.","Most workers are satisfied with their pay.","Few people know about the gig economy."],"a":0,"e":"Many appreciate freedom; a majority want more protection."},{"q":"What do economists suggest?","o":["A flexible arrangement that still provides basic security.","Returning completely to traditional jobs.","Allowing companies to set their own rules.","Ending the use of apps for work."],"a":0,"e":"They propose a middle path."}]},{"id":"bees6","t":"Saving the Bees","lv":"B2","g":[["pollination","tozlaşma"],["pesticide","tarım ilacı"],["mite","akar"],["meadow","çayır"],["pollinator","tozlayıcı"],["shelter","barınak"]],"p":["If you enjoyed an apple, a cup of coffee or a handful of almonds today, you can thank a bee. About one third of the food we eat depends on pollination, the process by which insects carry pollen from flower to flower so that plants can produce fruit and seeds. Honeybees get most of the attention, but thousands of wild bee species do the same job, often more efficiently.","Unfortunately, bees are in trouble. In many parts of the world, their numbers have fallen sharply over the last few decades. Scientists rarely blame a single cause. Instead, they point to a combination of pressures: the loss of wild flowers as farmland and cities expand, diseases carried by small creatures called mites, climate change that disturbs the timing of flowering, and the widespread use of certain pesticides.","The pesticide issue has been especially controversial. A group of chemicals known as neonicotinoids was found to damage bees' ability to find their way home. After years of research and campaigning, the European Union banned their use on outdoor crops in 2018. Farming organisations objected, arguing that the alternatives were more expensive and less effective, but many scientists welcomed the decision as a sign that evidence can influence policy.","Ordinary people can help, too. Planting flowers on balconies, leaving part of a garden uncut and avoiding chemical sprays all give bees food and shelter. Some cities have turned roofs into meadows, and a few have even created \"bee corridors\" of flowering plants along roads. Beekeeping has become popular as well, though experts warn that keeping hives in a city without enough flowers can actually harm wild species by creating competition for food.","Protecting bees is not only about saving an insect. It is about protecting the systems that feed us. Without pollinators, farmers would have to move pollen by hand, as already happens in parts of China, at enormous cost. As one ecologist puts it, \"Looking after bees is the cheapest insurance policy humanity has.\""],"q":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the text?","o":["To persuade readers to become beekeepers.","To describe how honey is produced.","To explain why bees matter and what threatens and helps them.","To criticise farmers who use chemicals."],"a":2,"e":"The text covers importance, threats and ways to help."},{"q":"According to paragraph 2, why are bee numbers falling?","o":["One disease has spread across the whole world.","Bees cannot survive in cities.","Several pressures combine, such as habitat loss, disease and pesticides.","Farmers have stopped planting flowers on purpose."],"a":2,"e":"Scientists rarely blame a single cause."},{"q":"What did neonicotinoids do to bees?","o":["They killed the flowers that bees feed on.","They damaged the bees' ability to find their way home.","They made bees more aggressive.","They reduced the amount of honey produced."],"a":1,"e":"The chemicals affected navigation."},{"q":"How did farming organisations react to the ban?","o":["They welcomed it as a victory for science.","They said the alternatives cost more and work less well.","They agreed to use fewer machines.","They asked governments to pay for beekeepers."],"a":1,"e":"They objected to the ban."},{"q":"Why do experts warn about beekeeping in cities?","o":["Too many hives can reduce the food available to wild bees.","Honeybees may attack people in crowded areas.","City flowers contain too many chemicals.","Hives are not allowed on rooftops."],"a":0,"e":"Hives can create competition for food."},{"q":"In paragraph 3, \"controversial\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["completely new","causing disagreement","extremely dangerous","widely forgotten"],"a":1,"e":"Farmers and scientists disagreed about the ban."},{"q":"What point does the example from China make?","o":["Chinese farmers have found a better method than bees.","Replacing natural pollination by hand would be extremely costly.","Bees are not needed in modern agriculture.","Hand pollination produces better fruit."],"a":1,"e":"It is done \"at enormous cost\"."}]},{"id":"fashion6","t":"The True Price of Fast Fashion","lv":"C1","g":[["outsource","dış kaynak kullanmak"],["toll","bedel, zarar"],["garment","giysi"],["disclose","açıklamak"],["thrive","gelişmek, serpilmek"],["greenwashing","yeşil badana"]],"p":["A T-shirt that costs less than a sandwich has become an ordinary sight on the high street. Fast fashion, the business model of producing inexpensive clothes in response to rapidly changing trends, has transformed the industry. Where designers once showed two collections a year, some brands now introduce thousands of new styles every week. For shoppers, the result is a seemingly endless supply of affordable novelty; for the planet and for the people who make the clothes, the picture is considerably less attractive.","The environmental toll is substantial. The fashion industry is estimated to account for around ten per cent of global carbon emissions, more than international flights and shipping combined. Producing a single cotton shirt can require thousands of litres of water, and the dyes used in textile factories frequently pollute rivers in the countries where manufacturing is concentrated. Synthetic fabrics such as polyester add a further problem: each wash releases microscopic plastic fibres that eventually find their way into the oceans.","The human cost is equally troubling. To keep prices low, brands often outsource production to countries where wages are minimal and regulations are weakly enforced. The 2013 collapse of the Rana Plaza factory in Bangladesh, which killed more than a thousand garment workers, exposed how unsafe conditions can be. Although the disaster prompted some reforms, campaigners maintain that many workers still earn too little to live decently, and that brands are reluctant to accept responsibility for their suppliers.","Consumers are not blameless, but placing the burden solely on them is misleading. Surveys indicate that a large share of shoppers claim to care about sustainability, yet few change their habits, partly because ethical alternatives are expensive and information is often vague. Terms such as \"eco-friendly\" or \"conscious\" are rarely regulated, which allows companies to engage in what critics call \"greenwashing\": promoting a modest environmental initiative to distract from damaging practices elsewhere.","Proposed solutions range from the individual to the systemic. Buying fewer, better-made items, repairing garments and choosing second-hand clothing can reduce personal impact, and the resale market is growing rapidly. More ambitious ideas include legislation that obliges companies to disclose their supply chains, taxes on non-recyclable textiles, and \"extended producer responsibility\" schemes that make brands pay for the disposal of what they sell. Whether such measures can succeed against an industry that thrives on volume remains an open question, but it is increasingly difficult to argue that the true price of a cheap shirt is the one printed on the label."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the text?","o":["To examine the environmental and human costs behind cheap clothing.","To advise readers on how to find the best bargains.","To trace the history of the fashion industry.","To argue that clothing prices should be regulated."],"a":0,"e":"The text describes environmental and human costs and possible remedies."},{"q":"What does the comparison with flights and shipping show?","o":["The industry's emissions are greater than those of both sectors combined.","Clothes are shipped further than any other product.","Aeroplanes are used to deliver most garments.","Transport is the biggest source of fashion's emissions."],"a":0,"e":"The text says fashion emits more than flights and shipping combined."},{"q":"What does the writer say about polyester?","o":["It uses more water than cotton to produce.","It is the cheapest fabric to recycle.","Washing it releases tiny plastic fibres that reach the oceans.","It cannot be dyed without polluting rivers."],"a":2,"e":"Microscopic fibres end up in the sea."},{"q":"What did the Rana Plaza collapse show?","o":["That brands had accepted responsibility for their suppliers.","How dangerous conditions in garment factories could be.","That wages in Bangladesh had risen quickly.","That most factories were carefully inspected."],"a":1,"e":"It exposed how unsafe conditions can be."},{"q":"What does \"placing the burden solely on them is misleading\" mean?","o":["Shoppers should not receive any information.","Consumers have no influence on companies.","Shoppers are too busy to change their habits.","Blaming only shoppers gives an incomplete picture."],"a":3,"e":"Companies and systems share responsibility."},{"q":"What is \"greenwashing\"?","o":["Washing clothes with environmentally friendly products.","Reducing production by using recycled fibres.","Advertising a small environmental effort to hide harmful practices.","Inventing new eco-labels for consumers."],"a":2,"e":"A modest initiative distracts from damaging practices."},{"q":"What does the final sentence suggest?","o":["Fashion labels should print higher prices.","Cheap clothes are always poorly made.","Shoppers should stop buying clothes entirely.","The price on the label hides the real costs of production."],"a":3,"e":"The \"true price\" is not the one on the label."}]},{"id":"placebo6","t":"The Power of the Placebo","lv":"C1","g":[["anaesthetist","anestezi uzmanı"],["expectation","beklenti"],["endorphin","endorfin"],["inert","etkisiz, etkin madde içermeyen"],["replication","yinelenme, tekrar doğrulama"],["fluctuate","dalgalanmak"]],"p":["In 1955, an American anaesthetist named Henry Beecher published a paper that would reshape medical thinking. Reviewing fifteen studies, he noticed that around a third of patients improved after receiving a treatment with no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill or an injection of salt water. This phenomenon, called the placebo effect, has since been demonstrated in conditions ranging from pain and depression to Parkinson's disease, and it continues to puzzle researchers.","The mechanism appears to involve expectation. When a person believes that a treatment will work, the brain can release natural painkillers called endorphins, and brain scans show measurable changes in activity. Importantly, the effect is not simply imagination: patients report real relief, and in some cases physical symptoms improve. The strength of a placebo varies with its presentation. Large pills work better than small ones, injections outperform tablets, and expensive brands seem more effective than cheaper but identical versions.","The ritual of care matters as well. Studies show that patients treated by a warm, attentive doctor respond better than those seen by a distant one, even when the treatment is identical. This suggests that part of what we call healing is relational: being listened to and reassured changes how we experience illness. Some researchers argue that a significant portion of the benefit attributed to certain therapies may stem from context rather than from the therapy itself.","The placebo effect presents doctors with an ethical dilemma. Prescribing a treatment that is known to be inert seems to involve deception, and deception undermines the trust on which medicine depends. Remarkably, a handful of trials have found that placebos can work even when patients are told openly that the pills contain no medicine. In one study of people with irritable bowel syndrome, those who knowingly took placebos reported greater improvement than those who received no treatment, although the sample was small and the results require replication.","Critics caution against exaggerating the phenomenon. Much of the apparent improvement in early studies, they note, may reflect natural recovery or the tendency of symptoms to fluctuate, rather than the pill's influence. Placebos cannot shrink tumours or cure infections, and their effects on subjective experiences such as pain are easier to detect than on measurable markers of disease. The most defensible conclusion is that placebo responses are genuine but limited, and that they offer a reminder that the mind and body are not separate systems. For medicine, the challenge lies in harnessing context, communication and expectation without compromising honesty."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main focus of the text?","o":["A history of medical research ethics.","Why doctors should prescribe sugar pills.","How painkillers work in the brain.","The nature, causes and limits of the placebo effect."],"a":3,"e":"The text describes the effect, explanations and criticism."},{"q":"What did Beecher notice in 1955?","o":["Most patients preferred injections to pills.","Salt water cured several serious diseases.","About a third of patients improved after inactive treatments.","Doctors prescribed sugar pills too often."],"a":2,"e":"Around a third improved without any active ingredient."},{"q":"How does paragraph 2 describe the placebo effect?","o":["As a real change influenced by what the patient expects.","As imagined relief without physical effects.","As the result of stronger chemicals in pills.","As something that happens only with expensive drugs."],"a":0,"e":"Expectation can trigger real, measurable changes."},{"q":"What does paragraph 3 suggest about a doctor's manner?","o":["Distant doctors prescribe more effective drugs.","Feeling listened to can improve how patients respond to treatment.","Warm doctors give patients faster diagnoses.","Patients prefer treatment by familiar doctors."],"a":1,"e":"Warm, attentive doctors get better responses."},{"q":"In paragraph 4, \"inert\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["having no active effect","extremely harmful","difficult to detect","recently developed"],"a":0,"e":"An inert treatment contains no active medicine."},{"q":"What qualification does the writer add about the open-label study?","o":["The sample was small and the results need confirming.","The patients did not understand the instructions.","The placebos were more expensive than normal pills.","Patients in the control group improved more."],"a":0,"e":"\"The sample was small and the results require replication.\""},{"q":"What point do critics make about early studies?","o":["Improvement may have come from natural recovery or changing symptoms.","Doctors failed to record the results.","Patients were not given enough time.","The treatments were too expensive."],"a":0,"e":"Symptoms fluctuate and people recover naturally."}]},{"id":"night6","t":"Reclaiming the Night Sky","lv":"C1","g":[["haze","pus, sis"],["nocturnal","gece aktif olan"],["melatonin","melatonin"],["carcinogenic","kanserojen"],["glare","göz kamaştırıcı ışık"],["indiscriminate","ayrım gözetmeyen"]],"p":["Few people alive today have seen the sky as their ancestors did. For most of human history, a clear night revealed thousands of stars and the pale band of the Milky Way; now, in many cities, only a few of the brightest stars are visible through a permanent orange haze. This phenomenon, known as light pollution, is the unintended consequence of a world that has become brighter each year, and scientists increasingly warn that its effects extend well beyond the disappointment of amateur astronomers.","The most obvious victims are wildlife. Migrating birds, which often navigate by the stars, are drawn towards illuminated skyscrapers and collide with them in vast numbers. Newly hatched sea turtles crawl towards streetlights rather than the moonlit sea, and moths circle lamps until they are exhausted or eaten. Even small amounts of artificial light can disrupt the breeding patterns of frogs and the pollination activity of nocturnal insects, with consequences that spread through entire ecosystems.","Humans are not immune. Our bodies depend on a daily rhythm regulated by light and darkness, and exposure to bright light in the evening suppresses melatonin, the hormone that prepares us for sleep. Epidemiological studies have linked night-shift work and bright bedrooms with sleep disorders, depression and, more tentatively, certain cancers. While causation is difficult to prove, the correlation has been sufficiently consistent for some health agencies to classify night-shift work as probably carcinogenic.","What makes light pollution unusual among environmental problems is how easily it can be solved. Unlike greenhouse gases, light leaves no residue; switch off a lamp, and the effect disappears instantly. Much of the illumination is simply wasted, directed upwards or sideways instead of onto the ground where it is needed. Simple measures such as shielding fixtures, using warmer-coloured bulbs, installing motion sensors and dimming lights after midnight can cut glare dramatically while saving energy and money.","Some places have already acted. Designated \"dark sky reserves\" in countries including New Zealand, Namibia and the United Kingdom restrict outdoor lighting to protect both the night sky and local wildlife, and they have become popular with tourists seeking stargazing holidays. Critics sometimes object that darker streets compromise safety, yet evidence for this is surprisingly weak; well-designed lighting that illuminates paths without blinding drivers appears to be at least as effective as indiscriminate brightness. The question, then, is less about choosing between light and dark than about using light with intention."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Stargazing should replace other leisure activities.","Excessive artificial light harms nature and people, but the problem can be reduced.","Cities should switch off all outdoor lighting.","Astronomers need better equipment to see the stars."],"a":1,"e":"The text describes harm and simple remedies."},{"q":"Why are sea turtles mentioned?","o":["They are misled by artificial light on their way to the sea.","They breed more successfully near cities.","They use the stars to navigate across oceans.","They are harmed by the chemicals in streetlights."],"a":0,"e":"Hatchlings crawl towards streetlights instead of the sea."},{"q":"In paragraph 3, \"suppresses\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["gradually increases","carefully measures","accidentally releases","reduces or prevents"],"a":3,"e":"Evening light lowers melatonin levels."},{"q":"What does the writer say about the link between light and cancer?","o":["It has been proved by many experiments.","It is a consistent association that is hard to prove as a cause.","It affects only people who work at night.","It has been dismissed by health agencies."],"a":1,"e":"Causation is hard to prove, but the correlation is consistent."},{"q":"Why is light pollution described as \"unusual\" among environmental problems?","o":["It affects only places with large populations.","It cannot be measured accurately.","It stops as soon as the light source is switched off.","It is more expensive to solve than other problems."],"a":2,"e":"Light leaves no residue."},{"q":"How does the writer respond to the safety objection?","o":["By saying that crime is lower in brightly lit areas.","By saying that drivers prefer streets with no lighting.","By saying that evidence for it is weak.","By saying that safety matters less than the environment."],"a":2,"e":"\"Evidence for this is surprisingly weak.\""},{"q":"What does the final sentence imply?","o":["Light and dark cannot coexist in cities.","Light should be used purposefully rather than excessively.","Governments should ban artificial light.","People prefer darkness to brightness."],"a":1,"e":"\"Using light with intention.\""}]}];var T={"kitchen6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin ana fikri nedir?","o":["Hazır yemekler çoğu şehirde evde yemek pişirmenin yerini alıyor.","Bir şehirdeki tüm mutfakları hayır kurumları işletmeli.","Topluluk mutfakları insanların iyi beslenmesine, tasarruf etmesine ve yeni insanlarla tanışmasına yardım eder.","Yemek yapmayı öğrenmek arkadaş edinmekten daha önemlidir."]},{"q":"Nora topluluk mutfağına neden katıldı?","o":["Profesyonel bir aşçı olmak istiyordu.","Yeni bir şehre taşınmıştı ve orada kimseyi tanımıyordu.","Hastanesi çalışanlardan birlikte yemek yapmalarını istemişti.","Büyükannesinin tariflerinden bıkmıştı."]},{"q":"Mutfaklar nasıl tasarruf sağlıyor?","o":["Her akşam restoranlardan ücretsiz yiyecek alıyorlar.","Sebzelerinin hepsini kendi bahçelerinde yetiştiriyorlar.","Üyelerden evden yiyecek getirmelerini istiyorlar.","Yiyecekleri toptan alıyor ve mağazaların satamadığı malzemeleri kullanıyorlar."]},{"q":"4. paragrafta \"spotless\" kelimesi en çok hangisine yakındır?","o":["özenle düzenlenmiş","tamamen temiz","yakın zamanda onarılmış","parlak aydınlatılmış"]},{"q":"Bazı gönüllüler grupla yemek yapmanın hangi sorununu dile getiriyor?","o":["Herkese yetecek kadar alet yok.","Üyeler yemeğin nasıl baharatlanacağı konusunda anlaşamıyor.","Yemeklerin hazırlanması çok uzun sürüyor.","Üyelerin çoğu yemek yapmayı bilmiyor."]},{"q":"Organizatörün son sözü neyi düşündürüyor?","o":["Yemekler pahalı olduğunda daha keyifli olur.","Üyelerin çoğu esas olarak yemek yapmayı öğrenmek için katılır.","İnsanlar birliktelik hissine en az yemek kadar değer veriyor.","Tek başına yemek yemek grupla yemekten daha sağlıklıdır."]}]},"swim6":{"q":[{"q":"Bazı yetişkinler neden hiç yüzmeyi öğrenmiyor?","o":["Yüzmenin yaşlılar için tehlikeli olduğuna inanıyorlar.","Yetişkin dersleri çoğu şehirde yok.","Spora hiç ilgileri yok.","Sudan uzakta yaşadılar, korktular ya da ders istemekten utandılar."]},{"q":"Tomas'ı sudan korkutan şey neydi?","o":["Bir yüzme havuzunda neredeyse boğuluyordu.","Bir okul dersinde onunla alay edilmişti.","Çocukken bir göle düşmüştü.","Amcası onu yüzmeye zorlamıştı."]},{"q":"Ders almaya karar vermesini ne sağladı?","o":["Kızı onu havuzda kendisine katılmaya çağırdı.","Doktoru daha çok egzersiz yapmasını önerdi.","Tatilde arkadaşlarıyla yüzmek istiyordu.","Ailesi korkusundan şikâyet ediyordu."]},{"q":"Lucia başlangıçta Tomas'a nasıl yardım etti?","o":["İlk gün ondan kısa bir mesafe yüzmesini istedi.","Ona her hafta özel ders verdi.","Basit alıştırmalarla yavaş ilerlemesine izin verdi.","Korkusunu görmezden gelmesini söyledi."]},{"q":"Lucia \"Yetişkinler neden diye sorar\" derken neyi kastediyor?","o":["Yetişkinler hata yapmaktan korkar.","Yetişkinler eğitmenin yöntemlerini sık sık sorgular.","Yetişkinlerin öğrenmesi çocuklardan daha uzun sürer.","Yetişkinler bir tekniğin nasıl işlediğini anlamak ister."]},{"q":"Tomas sonunda ne öneriyor?","o":["Denizde yüzmek havuzda yüzmekten kolaydır.","İlk adımı atmak işin en zor kısmıdır.","Yüzmeyi yalnızca küçük çocuklar öğrenmeli.","Yetişkinler için dersler ücretsiz olmalı."]}]},"tiny6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin ana fikri nedir?","o":["Minik evler konut sorununun en iyi çözümüdür.","Büyük evler modern şehirlerden kayboluyor.","Minik ev inşa etmek herkes için kolaydır.","Bazı insanlar pratik ve kişisel nedenlerle çok küçük evleri seçiyor."]},{"q":"Ivy minik evine taşınmadan önce ne yaptı?","o":["Eşyalarının çoğundan kurtuldu.","Arkadaşından evi tasarlamasını istedi.","Eski evini kiraya verdi.","Küçük alanlar için tasarlanmış mobilya aldı."]},{"q":"Minik evler mali açıdan neden cazip?","o":["Daha sonra daha yüksek fiyata satılabilirler.","Yerel vergi ödemek zorunda değiller.","Satın almak ve ısıtmak daha ucuz.","Geri dönüştürülmüş malzemeden yapılıyorlar."]},{"q":"2. paragrafta \"liberating\" kelimesi en çok hangisine yakındır?","o":["kayıp hissi yaratan","çok çaba gerektiren","özgürlük hissi veren","hayatı daha karmaşık yapan"]},{"q":"Minik evlerin yeriyle ilgili hangi zorluk belirtiliyor?","o":["Yerel kurallar yasal bir arazi bulmayı çoğu zaman zorlaştırıyor.","Kırsal bölgede çok az arazi var.","Komşular genellikle gürültüden şikâyet ediyor.","Minik evler kolay taşınamıyor."]},{"q":"Ivy küçük yaşamanın en büyük faydasını ne olarak görüyor?","o":["Daha büyük bir ev alacak kadar para biriktirmek.","Arkadaşlarından daha az eşyaya sahip olmak.","İş yerine daha yakın yaşamak.","Zamanını kendisi için önemli olan şeylere ayırma özgürlüğü."]}]},"dream6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin asıl amacı nedir?","o":["Rüyaları daha net hatırlamanın yolunu anlatmak.","İnsanların neden rüya gördüğüne dair farklı açıklamaları karşılaştırmak.","Rüyaların geleceği tahmin edebildiğini kanıtlamak.","Uyku evrelerini ayrıntılı biçimde tanımlamak."]},{"q":"Öğrencilerle yapılan deney neyi düşündürüyor?","o":["Akşam öğrenmek sabah öğrenmekten daha kolaydır.","Daha az uyuyan öğrenciler daha çok kelime hatırlar.","Rüya görmek problem çözme becerisini geliştirir.","Uyku, beynin yeni bilgiyi depolamasına yardım eder."]},{"q":"İkinci teoriye göre rahatsız edici rüyalar zamanla neden daha az üzücü oluyor?","o":["Beyin tatsız anıları tamamen siler.","İnsanlar rüya korkutucu hâle gelmeden uyanmayı öğrenir.","İnsanlar yaşlandıkça rüyalar kısalır.","Stresle bağlantılı kimyasallar REM uykusunda daha düşüktür."]},{"q":"4. paragrafta \"sceptical\" kelimesi en çok hangisine yakındır?","o":["yeni bir fikirden heyecanlanan","başkalarını dinlemek istemeyen","bir şeyin doğru olup olmadığından kuşku duyan","bir fikrin doğru olduğundan emin olan"]},{"q":"\"Aktivasyon-sentez\" görüşünü savunanlar neden mantıksız rüyalardan söz ediyor?","o":["Mantıksız rüyaların daha yaratıcı olduğuna inanıyorlar.","Bunun, rüyaların gizli bir anlamı olmadığını gösterdiğini düşünüyorlar.","Rüyaların nadir olduğunu göstermek istiyorlar.","Rüyaların rüya görenin kontrolünde olduğunu düşünüyorlar."]},{"q":"Yazar nasıl bir sonuca varıyor?","o":["Rüyalar yalnızca anlamsız beyin etkinliğidir.","Rüyaların gizemi çoktan çözülmüştür.","Rüya görmenin muhtemelen birkaç farklı amacı vardır.","Rüyalar yalnızca bellek için önemlidir."]},{"q":"Aşağıdakilerden hangisi rüyaların uyanık yaşam üzerindeki olası etkisi olarak anılmıyor?","o":["Rüyalar sanatçılara yeni fikirler verebilir.","Rüyalar bilim insanlarının sorun çözmesine yardım edebilir.","Rüyalar kişinin fiziksel sağlığını iyileştirebilir.","Rüyalar terapistlere konuşacak bir şey verebilir."]}]},"street6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin ana konusu nedir?","o":["Başarılı bir sokak sanatçısı nasıl olunur.","Sanayi şehirlerinde grafitinin tarihi.","Sokak sanatının değişen statüsü ve etrafındaki tartışmalar.","Dünyada duvar resmi için en iyi şehirler."]},{"q":"Bristol'deki sanayi bölgesinde ne oldu?","o":["Bölge çok popüler olduğu için turistler gelmeyi bıraktı.","Belediye duvarlara her türlü boyamayı yasakladı.","Duvar resimleri, sakinler bölgeyle gurur duyduğu için zararın azalmasını sağladı.","Sanatçılar taşındıktan sonra emlak fiyatları düştü."]},{"q":"Eleştirmenler yasal siparişler hakkında hangi endişeyi dile getiriyor?","o":["Sanatçılara işleri için çok az ödeniyor.","Resmen düzenlenen sanat bağımsız karakterini kaybedebilir.","Duvar resimleri çoğu zaman kötü boyanıyor.","Belediyeler yalnızca ünlü sanatçıları seçiyor."]},{"q":"\"Artwashing\" neyi ifade ediyor?","o":["Bir bölgeyi gözde hâle getirip emlak değerlerini yükseltmek için sanatı kullanmak.","Kamusal duvarlardaki grafitileri temizlemek.","Sokak sanatını özel koleksiyonculara satmak.","Ünlü resimleri binalara kopyalamak."]},{"q":"Mülkiyet neden bir sorun?","o":["Sanatçılar kamusal alanda yaptıkları işi imzalayamıyor.","Bina sahipleri resmi korumak için ödeme yapmak zorunda.","Halkın duvar resimlerini fotoğraflamasına izin yok.","Başkasının duvarına yapılan resim üzerinde kimin hakkı olduğu belirsiz."]},{"q":"Son paragraftaki ressam nasıl bir tutum sergiliyor?","o":["İnsanlar işinin üzerini boyadığında kızıyor.","Çalışmasının başkaları tarafından kapatılabileceğini kabul ediyor.","Duvara değil tuvale resim yapmayı tercih ediyor.","Duvarlara yalnızca ünlü sanatçıların resim yapması gerektiğine inanıyor."]},{"q":"Aşağıdakilerden hangisi yazarın yaklaşımını en iyi anlatır?","o":["Yazar bir taraf seçmeden iki görüşü de sunuyor.","Yazar yasal duvar resimlerini güçlü biçimde destekliyor.","Yazar sokak sanatının para israfı olduğunu düşünüyor.","Yazar sokak sanatının yasaklanması gerektiğini savunuyor."]}]},"gig6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin ana fikri nedir?","o":["Gig işi esneklik sunuyor ama ciddi bir güvencesizlik de yaratıyor.","Gig şirketleri çalışanlarına geleneksel işverenlerden daha iyi ödeme yapıyor.","Akıllı telefon uygulamaları tüm işleri kolaylaştırdı.","Hükümetler gig işlerinin çoğunu yasakladı."]},{"q":"Mara gig işini neden seviyor?","o":["Sınavları olduğunda çalışmayı bırakabiliyor.","Normal çalışanlardan daha çok kazanıyor.","Yöneticisi ders çalışmasına yardım ediyor.","Tam zamanlı çalışıyor ve ücretli izin alıyor."]},{"q":"Burada \"self-employed\" gig çalışanları için ne anlama geliyor?","o":["Her ay sabit maaş alıyorlar.","Asgari sayıda iş garanti ediliyor.","Yalnızca kendi ülkelerindeki şirketler için çalışıyorlar.","Kendi gelirlerinden kendileri sorumlu ve işverenden yan hak almıyorlar."]},{"q":"Bazı çalışanların gerçek ücreti neden asgari ücretin altına düşebiliyor?","o":["Şirketler her ayın sonunda ödemeleri düşürüyor.","Uygulamanın yüklenmesini beklemekle çok saat harcıyorlar.","Müşteriler teslimat çalışanlarına nadiren bahşiş veriyor.","Yakıt ve sigorta gibi masrafları kendileri ödemek zorundalar."]},{"q":"4. paragrafta \"entitle\" kelimesi en çok hangisine yakındır?","o":["birini bir şeyi yapmaya zorlamak","birine bir şey üzerinde hak vermek","birine unvan vermek","birinin bir şeyden kaçınmasına izin vermek"]},{"q":"Anketler ne gösteriyor?","o":["İnsanlar özgürlüğü seviyor ama daha fazla koruma da istiyor.","Çoğu insan gig işinin yasaklanmasını istiyor.","Çalışanların çoğu ücretinden memnun.","Çok az kişi gig ekonomisini biliyor."]},{"q":"Ekonomistler ne öneriyor?","o":["Yine de temel güvence sağlayan esnek bir düzenleme.","Tamamen geleneksel işlere dönmek.","Şirketlerin kendi kurallarını koymasına izin vermek.","İş için uygulama kullanımını sona erdirmek."]}]},"bees6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin asıl amacı nedir?","o":["Okurları arıcı olmaya ikna etmek.","Balın nasıl üretildiğini anlatmak.","Arıların neden önemli olduğunu, neyin onları tehdit ettiğini ve neyin yardım ettiğini açıklamak.","Kimyasal kullanan çiftçileri eleştirmek."]},{"q":"2. paragrafa göre arı sayıları neden düşüyor?","o":["Tek bir hastalık tüm dünyaya yayıldı.","Arılar şehirlerde hayatta kalamıyor.","Habitat kaybı, hastalık ve tarım ilaçları gibi birçok baskı bir araya geliyor.","Çiftçiler bilerek çiçek ekmeyi bıraktı."]},{"q":"Neonikotinoidler arılara ne yaptı?","o":["Arıların beslendiği çiçekleri öldürdü.","Arıların eve dönüş yolunu bulma yeteneğine zarar verdi.","Arıları daha saldırgan yaptı.","Üretilen bal miktarını azalttı."]},{"q":"Çiftçi kuruluşları yasağa nasıl tepki verdi?","o":["Bunu bilimin zaferi olarak karşıladılar.","Alternatiflerin daha pahalı olduğunu ve daha az etkili çalıştığını söylediler.","Daha az makine kullanmayı kabul ettiler.","Hükümetlerden arıcıların masraflarını karşılamasını istediler."]},{"q":"Uzmanlar şehirlerde arıcılık konusunda neden uyarıyor?","o":["Çok fazla kovan yabani arıların bulabileceği yiyeceği azaltabilir.","Bal arıları kalabalık yerlerde insanlara saldırabilir.","Şehirdeki çiçekler çok fazla kimyasal içeriyor.","Çatılarda kovan bulundurmak yasak."]},{"q":"3. paragrafta \"controversial\" kelimesi en çok hangisine yakındır?","o":["tamamen yeni","tartışmaya yol açan","son derece tehlikeli","çoktan unutulmuş"]},{"q":"Çin'den verilen örnek hangi noktayı vurguluyor?","o":["Çinli çiftçiler arılardan daha iyi bir yöntem buldu.","Doğal tozlaşmanın yerini elle tozlaştırmanın alması çok maliyetli olurdu.","Modern tarımda arılara ihtiyaç yok.","Elle tozlaştırma daha iyi meyve veriyor."]}]},"fashion6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin asıl amacı nedir?","o":["Ucuz giysilerin ardındaki çevresel ve insani bedelleri incelemek.","Okurlara en iyi indirimleri nasıl bulacaklarını önermek.","Moda endüstrisinin tarihini izlemek.","Giysi fiyatlarının düzenlenmesi gerektiğini savunmak."]},{"q":"Uçuşlar ve deniz taşımacılığıyla yapılan karşılaştırma neyi gösteriyor?","o":["Sektörün emisyonları, bu iki sektörün birlikte yaptığından fazladır.","Giysiler diğer her üründen daha uzağa gönderilir.","Giysilerin çoğunu teslim etmek için uçak kullanılır.","Modanın emisyonlarının en büyük kaynağı ulaşımdır."]},{"q":"Yazar polyester hakkında ne söylüyor?","o":["Üretimi için pamuktan daha fazla su kullanıyor.","Geri dönüştürülmesi en ucuz kumaş.","Yıkandığında okyanuslara ulaşan küçük plastik lifler salıyor.","Nehirleri kirletmeden boyanamıyor."]},{"q":"Rana Plaza'nın çöküşü neyi gösterdi?","o":["Markaların tedarikçilerinin sorumluluğunu üstlendiğini.","Konfeksiyon fabrikalarındaki koşulların ne kadar tehlikeli olabildiğini.","Bangladeş'te ücretlerin hızla arttığını.","Fabrikaların çoğunun dikkatle denetlendiğini."]},{"q":"\"Yükü yalnızca onlara yüklemek yanıltıcıdır\" ne anlama geliyor?","o":["Alışveriş yapanlar hiç bilgi almamalıdır.","Tüketicilerin şirketler üzerinde hiçbir etkisi yoktur.","Alışveriş yapanlar alışkanlıklarını değiştirmek için fazla meşguldür.","Yalnızca alışveriş yapanları suçlamak eksik bir tablo sunar."]},{"q":"\"Greenwashing\" nedir?","o":["Giysileri çevre dostu ürünlerle yıkamak.","Geri dönüştürülmüş lifler kullanarak üretimi azaltmak.","Zararlı uygulamaları gizlemek için küçük bir çevre çabasını öne çıkarmak.","Tüketiciler için yeni çevre etiketleri icat etmek."]},{"q":"Son cümle neyi düşündürüyor?","o":["Moda etiketleri daha yüksek fiyatlar yazmalı.","Ucuz giysiler her zaman kötü yapılmıştır.","Alışveriş yapanlar giysi almayı tamamen bırakmalı.","Etiketteki fiyat, üretimin gerçek maliyetlerini gizliyor."]}]},"placebo6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin ana odağı nedir?","o":["Tıbbi araştırma etiğinin tarihi.","Doktorların neden şeker hapı yazması gerektiği.","Ağrı kesicilerin beyinde nasıl çalıştığı.","Plasebo etkisinin doğası, nedenleri ve sınırları."]},{"q":"Beecher 1955'te neyi fark etti?","o":["Hastaların çoğu iğneyi hapa tercih etti.","Tuzlu su birkaç ciddi hastalığı iyileştirdi.","Hastaların yaklaşık üçte biri etkisiz tedavilerden sonra iyileşti.","Doktorlar çok sık şeker hapı yazdı."]},{"q":"2. paragraf plasebo etkisini nasıl tanımlıyor?","o":["Hastanın beklentisinden etkilenen gerçek bir değişim olarak.","Fiziksel etkisi olmayan hayali bir rahatlama olarak.","Haplardaki daha güçlü kimyasalların sonucu olarak.","Yalnızca pahalı ilaçlarda görülen bir şey olarak."]},{"q":"3. paragraf doktorun tavrı hakkında ne düşündürüyor?","o":["Mesafeli doktorlar daha etkili ilaçlar yazar.","Dinlendiğini hissetmek, hastaların tedaviye yanıtını iyileştirebilir.","Sıcakkanlı doktorlar hastalara daha hızlı teşhis koyar.","Hastalar tanıdık doktorlarca tedavi edilmeyi tercih eder."]},{"q":"4. paragrafta \"inert\" kelimesi en çok hangisine yakındır?","o":["etkin bir etkisi olmayan","son derece zararlı","tespit edilmesi zor","yakın zamanda geliştirilmiş"]},{"q":"Yazar, hastaların placebo aldıklarını bildiği çalışma hakkında hangi sınırlamayı ekliyor?","o":["Örneklem küçüktü ve sonuçların doğrulanması gerekiyor.","Hastalar yönergeleri anlamadı.","Plasebolar normal haplardan daha pahalıydı.","Kontrol grubundaki hastalar daha çok iyileşti."]},{"q":"Eleştirmenler erken çalışmalar hakkında hangi noktayı vurguluyor?","o":["İyileşme, doğal iyileşmeden ya da değişen belirtilerden kaynaklanmış olabilir.","Doktorlar sonuçları kaydetmeyi başaramadı.","Hastalara yeterli süre tanınmadı.","Tedaviler çok pahalıydı."]}]},"night6":{"q":[{"q":"Metnin ana fikri nedir?","o":["Yıldız gözlemi diğer boş zaman etkinliklerinin yerini almalı.","Aşırı yapay ışık doğaya ve insanlara zarar veriyor, ancak sorun azaltılabilir.","Şehirler tüm dış aydınlatmayı kapatmalı.","Astronomların yıldızları görmek için daha iyi ekipmana ihtiyacı var."]},{"q":"Deniz kaplumbağalarından neden söz ediliyor?","o":["Denize giderken yapay ışık tarafından yanıltılıyorlar.","Şehirlerin yakınında daha başarılı ürüyorlar.","Okyanusları geçerken yıldızlardan yararlanıyorlar.","Sokak lambalarındaki kimyasallardan zarar görüyorlar."]},{"q":"3. paragrafta \"suppresses\" kelimesi en çok hangisine yakındır?","o":["yavaş yavaş artırır","dikkatle ölçer","yanlışlıkla salar","azaltır veya engeller"]},{"q":"Yazar ışık ile kanser arasındaki bağlantı hakkında ne söylüyor?","o":["Birçok deneyle kanıtlanmıştır.","Tutarlı bir ilişki ama neden-sonuç olarak kanıtlanması zor.","Yalnızca gece çalışan kişileri etkiler.","Sağlık kuruluşlarınca reddedilmiştir."]},{"q":"Işık kirliliği çevre sorunları arasında neden \"alışılmadık\" olarak tanımlanıyor?","o":["Yalnızca nüfusu kalabalık yerleri etkiliyor.","Doğru biçimde ölçülemiyor.","Işık kaynağı kapatılır kapatılmaz sona eriyor.","Çözmesi diğer sorunlardan daha pahalı."]},{"q":"Yazar güvenlik itirazına nasıl yanıt veriyor?","o":["Parlak aydınlatılan bölgelerde suçun daha az olduğunu söyleyerek.","Sürücülerin aydınlatmasız sokakları tercih ettiğini söyleyerek.","Bunun kanıtının zayıf olduğunu söyleyerek.","Güvenliğin çevreden daha az önemli olduğunu söyleyerek."]},{"q":"Son cümle neyi ima ediyor?","o":["Işık ve karanlık şehirlerde bir arada bulunamaz.","Işık aşırıya kaçmadan, amaca uygun kullanılmalı.","Hükümetler yapay ışığı yasaklamalı.","İnsanlar karanlığı aydınlığa tercih ediyor."]}]}};window.READINGS=(window.READINGS||[]).concat(A);window.READINGS_TR=Object.assign(window.READINGS_TR||{},T)})();
